<<<
trilogy (Lit.); ‘three pieces’. In ancient Athens, there were dramatic competitions at which each dramatist presented three plays, originally giving successive parts of the same legend; the extant Agamemnon, Choephoroe, & Eumenides, of Aeschylus formed a trilogy, &, with the addition of the lost Proteus, a tetralogy (see above). Later trilogies
===
*trilogy* (Lit.); 'three pieces'. In ancient Athens, there were dramatic competitions at which each dramatist presented three plays, originally giving successive parts of the same legend; the extant *Agamemnon, Choephoroe,* & *Eumenides,* of Aeschylus formed a trilogy, &, with the addition of the lost *Proteus*, a *tetralogy* (see above). Later trilogies
>>>

<<<
TRIPLET illustrious far shall be he may be born— who shall set thee free, changed & worn on thy brow replaced; replenishing thy morn, long with clouds defaced, from Avernus risen, breathe who are debased mind in prison. were connected not necessarily by a common subject, but by being works of the same poet presented on the same occasion. In modern use the word is applied to a work such as Shakspere’s Henry IV, comprising three separate plays, or to a novel &c. with two sequels.
===
+ were connected not necessarily by a common subject, but by being works of the same poet presented on the same occasion. In modern use the word is applied to a work such as Shakspere's *Henry IV*, comprising three separate plays, or to a novel &c. with two sequels.
>>>

